Day 3 – Disable Direct SSH Root Login on App Servers (DevOps / Linux)

Objective

Following xFusionCorp Industries security audits, disable direct root SSH login on all app servers in the Stratos Datacenter and apply the change to the running SSH service.

Environment
Server    User     Access
stapp01   tony     SSH from jump host (sudo / wheel group)
stapp02   steve    SSH from jump host
stapp03   banner   SSH from jump host

Configuration Change
File:     /etc/ssh/sshd_config
Setting:  PermitRootLogin no

Steps Performed (repeated on each server)
1. Connected from the jump host: ssh <user>@<server> (accepted the host fingerprint).
2. Confirmed identity with whoami and id (user in the wheel group, so sudo is available).
3. Opened the config: sudo nano /etc/ssh/sshd_config
4. Set PermitRootLogin no (uncommented and changed from yes).
5. Validated the syntax: sudo sshd -t (no output = valid).
6. Restarted the service: sudo systemctl restart sshd
7. Verified: sudo systemctl status sshd showed active (running) and listening on port 22.

Troubleshooting
- sudo systemctl restart ssh failed with "Unit ssh.service not found". These servers are RHEL-family, where the service is named sshd, not ssh.
- On a final check of stapp01, a second PermitRootLogin yes entry was found near the end of the file. It was changed to no so no conflicting value remained, and the config was re-validated with sshd -t.

Important Concepts Learned
- PermitRootLogin: sshd directive controlling whether root can log in over SSH.
- sshd -t: Tests the configuration for errors before restarting, avoiding lockouts.
- Restart required: Changes to sshd_config only take effect after the service is restarted/reloaded.
- Service names differ: sshd (RHEL/CentOS/Rocky) vs ssh (Debian/Ubuntu).
- Least privilege: Admins log in as normal users and use sudo, which gives an audit trail and removes the root brute-force target.

Key Takeaway
Disabling direct root SSH login hardens servers. Always validate with sshd -t, restart the right service, and verify its status.

Security Note: Keep a working non-root sudo user and an open session while testing, so a config mistake doesn't lock you out.
